PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L12U4.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":12,"cefr":"C2","unit":4,"theme":"The Future of Humanity","essential_question":"Can we predict the future, or only prepare for it?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1","title":"By 2050, We Will Have…; By 2000, They Would Have…: Future Perfect and Predictions from the Past","objectives":["Students will be able to use the future perfect simple, continuous and passive to look back from a point in the future.","Students will be able to report past predictions with would, would have and was to have been.","I can write \"By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion.\"","I can write \"In the 1960s, experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built cities on the Moon.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام المستقبل التام البسيط والمستمر والمجهول للنظر إلى الوراء من نقطة في المستقبل.","سيتمكن الطلاب من نقل تنبؤات الماضي بـ would وwould have وwas to have been.","أستطيع أن أكتب By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion.","أستطيع أن أكتب In the 1960s, experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built cities on the Moon."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"forecast","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfɔrˌkæst/","definition":"a statement about what is likely to happen, based on information","arabic":"توقّع / تنبؤ","example":"The weather forecast predicts rain.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W01"},{"word":"projection","pos":"noun","ipa":"/prəˈdʒɛkʃən/","definition":"an estimate of a future amount based on present trends","arabic":"إسقاط / تقدير مستقبلي","example":"Population projections suggest growth until the 2080s.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W02"},{"word":"trajectory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/trəˈdʒɛktɚi/","definition":"the path or direction of development over time","arabic":"مسار","example":"The country's economic trajectory looks positive.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W03"},{"word":"scenario","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sɪˈnɛrioʊ/","definition":"a possible situation or sequence of events in the future","arabic":"سيناريو","example":"In the worst-case scenario, sea levels will rise by a meter.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"The future perfect (will have + past participle) looks back from a point in the future: By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion. (The reaching happens before 2050.) The future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing) stresses duration up to that point: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years. Passive: By 2030, the new railway will have been completed.\nTo report predictions made in the past, use the future in the past: would + base verb (The writer foresaw that people would be able to call without wires.); would be + -ing (They imagined that we would be traveling in flying cars.); would have + past participle – a future perfect seen from the past (Experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built bases on the Moon.).\nwas / were to have been + past participle = something was planned or arranged but did not happen: The first lunar settlement was to have been constructed in the 1990s. Time expressions: by + time (by 2050, by then, by the time…), for + duration with the continuous form. Don't use will after by the time in the time clause: By the time we arrive (NOT (not: will arrive)), the meeting will have started.\nwill have + past participle (completed before a future point)\nwill have been + -ing (duration up to a future point) • will have been + past participle (passive)\npast predictions: would + base • would be + -ing • would have + past participle\nwas / were to have been + past participle (planned – didn't happen)","examples":["By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion.","By then, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years.","By 2030, the new railway will have been completed.","The writer foresaw that people would be able to call without wires.","In the 1960s, experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built bases on the Moon.","They imagined that we would be traveling in flying cars.","The lunar settlement was to have been constructed in the 1990s.","By the time you read this letter, I will have graduated."],"common_errors":["✗ By 2050, the population will reach ten billion already. → ✓ By 2050, the population will have reached ten billion. (Completed before 2050 → future perfect.)","✗ By 2050, astronauts will have lived in space for fifty years continuously. (stress on duration) → ✓ By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years. (Duration → continuous.)","✗ In 1960, experts believed that by 2000 humans will have built Moon cities. → ✓ In 1960, experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built Moon cities. (Past prediction → would have.)","✗ By the time we will arrive, the film will have started. → ✓ By the time we arrive, the film will have started. (Present tense after by the time.)"],"arabic":"المستقبل التام (will have + التصريف الثالث) ينظر إلى الخلف من نقطة مستقبلية: By 2050, the population will have reached nearly ten billion.\nالمستقبل التام المستمر (will have been + -ing) يؤكد المدة: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years.\nلنقل تنبؤات الماضي: would + المصدر، وwould be + -ing، وwould have + التصريف الثالث: Experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built Moon bases.\nwas to have been + التصريف الثالث لما خُطط له ولم يحدث، ولا نستخدم will بعد by the time."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “By 2050, We Will Have…; By 2000, They Would Have…: Future Perfect and Predictions from the Past”. Let’s learn future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U4-LS1 | By 2050, We Will Have…; By 2000, They Would Have…: Future Perfect and Predictions from the Past","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Imagining Tomorrow”: In 1900, an American magazine published a list of predictions for the year 2000. Some now seem amusing, but others were remarkably accurate: the writer foresaw that people would be able to talk to friends on other continents without wires. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"In 1900, an American magazine published a list of predictions for the year 2000. Some now seem amusing, but others were remarkably accurate: the writer foresaw that people would be able to talk to friends on other continents without wires.","visual":"Image GV-L12-U4-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"The future perfect (will have + past participle) looks back from a point in the future: By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion. (The reaching happens before 2050.) The future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing) stresses duration up to that point: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years. Passive: By 2030, the new railway will have been completed. To report predictions made in the past, use the future in the past: would + base verb (The writer foresaw that people would be able to call without wires.); would be + -ing (They imagined that we would be traveling in flying cars.); would have + past participle – a future perfect seen from the past (Experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built bases on the Moon.).","on_screen":"will have + past participle (completed before a future point) • will have been + -ing (duration up to a future point) • will have been + past participle (passive) • past predictions: would + base • would be + -ing • would have + past participle","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion. By then, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years. By 2030, the new railway will have been completed.","on_screen":"By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion. / By then, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years. / By 2030, the new railway will have been completed.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “By 2050, the population will reach ten billion already.” That’s not right. We say: “By 2050, the population will have reached ten billion.” Completed before 2050 → future perfect.","on_screen":"✗ By 2050, the population will reach ten billion already.   ✓ By 2050, the population will have reached ten billion.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"By 2050, the population will have reached ten billion.\" When does the reaching happen? … Think! … Before or by 2050. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","المستقبل التام (will have + التصريف الثالث) ينظر إلى الخلف من نقطة مستقبلية: By 2050, the population will have reached nearly ten billion.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: By 2050, the population will reach ten billion already. — الصحيح: By 2050, the population will have reached ten billion.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Imagining Tomorrow","body":"In 1900, an American magazine published a list of predictions for the year 2000. Some now seem amusing, but others were remarkably accurate: the writer foresaw that people would be able to talk to friends on other continents without wires. Sixty years later, many experts were convinced that, by the year 2000, humans would have built permanent bases on the Moon. That future never arrived. Several lunar settlements were to have been constructed decades ago; in all likelihood, the first permanent one is still years away.\nPredicting the future is notoriously difficult. Yet some trends make certain outcomes highly probable. According to United Nations projections, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion by 2050, and there is every chance that people will live longer than ever before. By then, astronauts will have been living aboard space stations continuously for half a century.\nSaudi Arabia is also looking toward space. In 2023, Rayyanah Barnawi became the first Saudi woman to travel into space, alongside her colleague Ali AlQarni. The chances are that their mission will be remembered as the beginning of a new era for Arab science.\nFuturists often describe two kinds of scenario. In optimistic visions, technology helps humanity cure diseases, end poverty and protect the planet. In pessimistic ones, climate change and conflict push civilization toward collapse. Neither outcome is inevitable. As Ms. Huda tells her students, \"The future is not something that happens to us; it is something we build.\"\nWhat is bound to matter most are the choices made by today's young people, many of whom will still be working and leading their communities in 2070. By then, they will have seen changes that we can hardly imagine.","word_count":283,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"By 2050, the world's population ___ nearly ten billion.","options":["will reach already","will have reached","would have reached","has reached"],"answer":"will have reached","explanation":"Future perfect (will have done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام."},{"q":"By 2050, astronauts ___ aboard space stations for fifty years.","options":["have been living","will have been living","will have living","will be lived"],"answer":"will have been living","explanation":"Future perfect continuous (will have been doing).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المستمر."},{"q":"In 1900, the writer foresaw that people ___ to talk without wires.","options":["are able","will be able","have been able","would be able"],"answer":"would be able","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"In the 1960s, experts believed that by 2000 humans ___ permanent bases on the Moon.","options":["will have built","had built","would have built","would build up"],"answer":"would have built","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"The first lunar settlement ___ in the 1990s, but the project was canceled.","options":["was to have been constructed","will have been constructed","is to have been constructed","was to construct"],"answer":"was to have been constructed","explanation":"was / were to have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"was to have been."},{"q":"According to the transport ministry, by the end of the decade the national railway network ______, connecting every major city in the Kingdom.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["will have been completed","will have completed","is completing by then","would have completed"],"answer":"will have been completed","explanation":"Future perfect passive (will have been done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المجهول."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with the correct future form. Word bank: will have been working, will have graduated, would be traveling","items":[{"q":"By the time you read this letter, I ______ (graduate) from university.","options":[],"answer":"will have graduated","explanation":"Future perfect (will have done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام."},{"q":"By next June, my father ______ (work) at the hospital for thirty years.","options":[],"answer":"will have been working","explanation":"Future perfect continuous (will have been doing).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المستمر."},{"q":"In 1980, many people thought that by 2000 we ______ (travel) in flying cars.","options":[],"answer":"would be traveling","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"By the time we will arrive, the conference will have started.","options":[],"answer":"By the time we arrive, the conference will have started.","explanation":"will arrive → arrive. Present tense after by the time / when.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: will arrive → arrive. by the time."},{"q":"In 1960, scientists believed that by 2000 humans will have reached Mars.","options":[],"answer":"In 1960, scientists believed that by 2000 humans would have reached Mars.","explanation":"will have reached → would have reached. Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: will have reached → would have reached. المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"By 2030, the new stadium will have built.","options":[],"answer":"By 2030, the new stadium will have been built.","explanation":"will have built → will have been built. Future perfect passive (will have been done).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: will have built → will have been built. المستقبل التام المجهول."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Look forward and back.","items":[{"q":"Write about yourself in ten years with \"will have…\".","options":[],"answer":"In ten years, I will have finished my engineering degree.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a long activity with \"will have been + -ing\".","options":[],"answer":"By 2030, my grandmother will have been teaching the Quran for fifty years.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Report a prediction from the past with \"would\".","options":[],"answer":"My grandfather thought that phones would never replace letters.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Report a past prediction with \"would have\".","options":[],"answer":"In 1990, my father believed that by 2020 cars would have stopped using petrol.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a canceled plan with \"was to have been\".","options":[],"answer":"The new bridge was to have been finished last year.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A letter to the future.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences to your future self in 2040 with future perfect forms.","options":[],"answer":"By the time you read this, you will have finished university. You will have been working for at least ten years.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences about predictions that people made in the past.","options":[],"answer":"In the 1950s, people imagined that robots would be doing all the housework by 2000. They also believed that we would have built hotels on the Moon.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Predict a change your little brother will see, beginning \"By the time…\".","options":[],"answer":"By the time my little brother finishes school, cars will have become fully electric.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin & Speak","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Work in teams of four. Player 1 spins the wheel.","Make a correct sentence about the unit theme using the cue word and future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen).","The team checks the sentence with the grammar box. Correct = 1 point; an extra detail = 1 bonus point.","Pass the spinner clockwise. Play for 6 minutes; the team with most points wins."],"data":[{"label":"will have reached"},{"label":"will have been living"},{"label":"would be able to"},{"label":"would have built"},{"label":"was to have been constructed"},{"label":"there is every chance"},{"label":"in all likelihood"},{"label":"is bound to"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"By 2035, scientists ___ a cure for several diseases, according to some forecasts.","options":["have found","will find already","would have found","will have found"],"answer":"will have found","explanation":"Future perfect (will have done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام."},{"q":"By December, she ___ the novel for three years.","options":["will have writing","will have been writing","has been writing","will be written"],"answer":"will have been writing","explanation":"Future perfect continuous (will have been doing).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المستمر."},{"q":"By 2030, millions of solar panels ___ across the region.","options":["are installed by","will be install","will have been installed","will have installed"],"answer":"will have been installed","explanation":"Future perfect passive (will have been done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المجهول."},{"q":"In 1970, experts predicted that by 2000 robots ___ most factory work.","options":["would doing","are doing","will be doing","would be doing"],"answer":"would be doing","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"The space hotel ___ in 2020, but the company ran out of money.","options":["was to have opened","will have opened","was to opening","is to have opened"],"answer":"was to have opened","explanation":"was / were to have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"was to have been."},{"q":"","options":["When you returned from your trip, we will have moved to our new house.","When you return from your trip, we will moved to our new house.","When you return from your trip, we will have moved to our new house.","When you will return from your trip, we will have moved to our new house."],"answer":"When you return from your trip, we will have moved to our new house.","explanation":"Present tense after by the time / when.","explanation_ar":"by the time."},{"q":"","options":["They believed that by 2000 people would have colonize the Moon.","They believed that by 2000 people would colonized the Moon.","They believed that by 2000 people would have colonized the Moon.","They believed that by 2000 people will have colonized the Moon."],"answer":"They believed that by 2000 people would have colonized the Moon.","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"If current trends continue, by 2060 the average person in many countries ______ more than eighty years, a dramatic increase from a century earlier.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["will have been living","will have been lived","has been living","would have been living"],"answer":"will have been living","explanation":"Future perfect continuous (will have been doing).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المستمر."},{"q":"When the space agency was founded in the 1950s, its scientists assumed that, within fifty years, astronauts ______ regular trips to other planets.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["will be making","would be making","are making","would make up"],"answer":"would be making","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"\"The new library was to have been finished last year.\" What happened?","options":["It wasn't finished as planned.","It was never planned.","It will be finished next year for sure.","It was finished last year."],"answer":"It wasn't finished as planned.","explanation":"was / were to have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"was to have been."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A museum exhibition called \"Yesterday's Tomorrow\": a 1900 magazine page with drawings of flying machines; a 1960s poster of a Moon city; a modern screen showing the International Space Station and a Saudi astronaut in a spacesuit. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A museum exhibition called \"Yesterday's Tomorrow\": a 1900 magazine page with drawings of flying machines; a 1960s poster of a Moon city; a modern screen showing the International Space Station and a Saudi astronaut in a spacesuit"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen), with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…)"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows forecast (a statement about what is likely to happen, based on information); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing forecast: a statement about what is likely to happen, based on information"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows projection (an estimate of a future amount based on present trends); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing projection: an estimate of a future amount based on present trends"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows trajectory (the path or direction of development over time); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing trajectory: the path or direction of development over time"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows scenario (a possible situation or sequence of events in the future); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing scenario: a possible situation or sequence of events in the future"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin & Speak”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin & Speak"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W01","script":"forecast. forecast. The weather forecast predicts rain.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W02","script":"projection. projection. Population projections suggest growth until the 2080s.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W03","script":"trajectory. trajectory. The country's economic trajectory looks positive.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-W04","script":"scenario. scenario. In the worst-case scenario, sea levels will rise by a meter.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-T1","script":"In 1900, an American magazine published a list of predictions for the year 2000. Some now seem amusing, but others were remarkably accurate: the writer foresaw that people would be able to talk to friends on other continents without wires. Sixty years later, many experts were convinced that, by the year 2000, humans would have built permanent bases on the Moon. That future never arrived. Several lunar settlements were to have been constructed decades ago; in all likelihood, the first permanent one is still years away. Predicting the future is notoriously difficult. Yet some trends make certain outcomes highly probable. According to United Nations projections, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion by 2050, and there is every chance that people will live longer than ever before. By then, astronauts will have been living aboard space stations continuously for half a century. Saudi Arabia is also looking toward space. In 2023, Rayyanah Barnawi became the first Saudi woman to travel into space, alongside her colleague Ali AlQarni. The chances are that their mission will be remembered as the beginning of a new era for Arab science. Futurists often describe two kinds of scenario. In optimistic visions, technology helps humanity cure diseases, end poverty and protect the planet. In pessimistic ones, climate change and conflict push civilization toward collapse. Neither outcome is inevitable. As Ms. Huda tells her students, \"The future is not something that happens to us; it is something we build.\" What is bound to matter most are the choices made by today's young people, many of whom will still be working and leading their communities in 2070. By then, they will have seen changes that we can hardly imagine.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “By 2050, We Will Have…; By 2000, They Would Have…: Future Perfect and Predictions from the Past”. Let’s learn future perfect simple (will have + past participle: By 2050, the population will have reached…); future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years); future perfect passive (will have been built); future in the past for predictions made in the past: would + base (people would be able to…), would be + -ing, would have + past participle (by 2000, humans would have built…), was / were to have been + past participle (an arrangement that didn't happen) together. Listen to the start of “Imagining Tomorrow”: In 1900, an American magazine published a list of predictions for the year 2000. Some now seem amusing, but others were remarkably accurate: the writer foresaw that people would be able to talk to friends on other continents without wires. Look at the green words. The future perfect (will have + past participle) looks back from a point in the future: By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion. (The reaching happens before 2050.) The future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing) stresses duration up to that point: By 2050, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years. Passive: By 2030, the new railway will have been completed. To report predictions made in the past, use the future in the past: would + base verb (The writer foresaw that people would be able to call without wires.); would be + -ing (They imagined that we would be traveling in flying cars.); would have + past participle – a future perfect seen from the past (Experts believed that by 2000 humans would have built bases on the Moon.). Here are some examples. By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion. By then, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years. By 2030, the new railway will have been completed. Be careful! Some learners say: “By 2050, the population will reach ten billion already.” That’s not right. We say: “By 2050, the population will have reached ten billion.” Completed before 2050 → future perfect. Quick check: \"By 2050, the population will have reached ten billion.\" When does the reaching happen? … Think! … Before or by 2050. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"By 2050, the world's population will have reached nearly ten billion.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2","title":"There's Every Chance; In All Likelihood: Expressing Degrees of Probability","objectives":["Students will be able to express fine degrees of probability with idiomatic and formal expressions.","Students will be able to use the correct grammar after each expression (that-clause, to-infinitive, of + -ing).","I can say \"There's every chance that people will live longer.\"","I can say \"The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2030.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التعبير عن درجات دقيقة من الاحتمال بتعبيرات اصطلاحية ورسمية.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام القواعد الصحيحة بعد كل تعبير (جملة that أو to + المصدر أو of + -ing).","أستطيع أن أقول There's every chance that people will live longer.","أستطيع أن أقول The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2030."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"speculative","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈspɛkjələtɪv/","definition":"based on guesses rather than facts","arabic":"تخميني","example":"His ideas about time travel are highly speculative.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W01"},{"word":"extrapolate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɛkˈstræpəˌleɪt/","definition":"to estimate future results by extending present trends","arabic":"يستقرئ / يمدّ الاتجاه","example":"Scientists extrapolate from current data to predict temperatures.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W02"},{"word":"inevitable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪˈnɛvətəbəl/","definition":"certain to happen and impossible to avoid","arabic":"حتمي","example":"Change is inevitable.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W03"},{"word":"utopia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/juˈtoʊpiə/","definition":"an imagined perfect society","arabic":"المدينة الفاضلة","example":"Some writers imagine a utopia without poverty.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Certain or almost certain: is bound to / is sure to + verb (Technology is bound to change the way we work.); it's a foregone conclusion that (the result is already known); undoubtedly. Very probable: there's every chance that (People will live longer – there's every chance.); in all likelihood / in all probability (In all likelihood, the first Mars landing will be in the 2030s.); the chances / odds are that; it's a safe bet that; stand a good chance of + -ing (She stands a good chance of winning).\nPossible: might conceivably / could potentially (Robots could potentially replace some teachers.); there's a chance that. Unlikely: there's little / a slim chance that; it's doubtful whether / that; the odds are against + noun / -ing (The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2030.); presumably means \"I assume, but I'm not certain\". Impossible: there's no chance / no way that (informal).\nGrammar after each expression: bound to / sure to / likely to + base verb; there's every / little chance (that) + clause; in all likelihood, + clause; stand a good chance of + -ing; the odds are against + noun / -ing. Don't mix patterns: (not: There's every chance to people live longer) → There's every chance that people will live longer.\ncertain: is bound to • is sure to • it's a foregone conclusion that\nprobable: there's every chance that • in all likelihood • the odds are that • stand a good chance of + -ing\npossible: might conceivably • could potentially • there's a chance that\nunlikely / impossible: there's little chance that • it's doubtful whether • the odds are against + -ing • no chance","examples":["Technology is bound to change the way we work.","There's every chance that people will live longer than ever before.","In all likelihood, the first crewed Mars landing will take place in the 2030s or later.","The odds are that electric cars will dominate within twenty years.","Our team stands a good chance of winning the robotics competition.","Robots could potentially replace some routine jobs.","There's little chance that humans will live on Mars by 2030.","It's doubtful whether flying cars will ever be common."],"common_errors":["✗ There's every chance to people live longer. → ✓ There's every chance that people will live longer. (every chance + that-clause.)","✗ She stands a good chance to win. → ✓ She stands a good chance of winning. (of + -ing.)","✗ Technology is bound changing our lives. → ✓ Technology is bound to change our lives. (bound to + base verb.)","✗ The odds are against that humans live on Mars soon. → ✓ The odds are against humans living on Mars soon. (against + noun / -ing.)"],"arabic":"اليقين: is bound to / is sure to / it's a foregone conclusion that.\nالاحتمال الكبير: there's every chance that / in all likelihood / the odds are that / stand a good chance of + -ing.\nالإمكان: might conceivably / could potentially، وعدم الترجيح: there's little chance that / it's doubtful whether / the odds are against + -ing، والاستحالة: there's no chance.\nانتبه للقواعد بعد كل تعبير: bound to + المصدر، every chance that + جملة، stand a good chance of + -ing."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “There's Every Chance; In All Likelihood: Expressing Degrees of Probability”. Let’s learn probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U4-LS2 | There's Every Chance; In All Likelihood: Expressing Degrees of Probability","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: In all likelihood, the population will peak this century. There's little chance that humans will live on Mars by 2030. What are the chances that robots will replace teachers?","on_screen":"In all likelihood, the population will peak this century. | There's little chance that humans will live on Mars by 2030. | What are the chances that robots will replace teachers?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Certain or almost certain: is bound to / is sure to + verb (Technology is bound to change the way we work.); it's a foregone conclusion that (the result is already known); undoubtedly. Very probable: there's every chance that (People will live longer – there's every chance.); in all likelihood / in all probability (In all likelihood, the first Mars landing will be in the 2030s.); the chances / odds are that; it's a safe bet that; stand a good chance of + -ing (She stands a good chance of winning). Possible: might conceivably / could potentially (Robots could potentially replace some teachers.); there's a chance that. Unlikely: there's little / a slim chance that; it's doubtful whether / that; the odds are against + noun / -ing (The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2030.); presumably means \"I assume, but I'm not certain\". Impossible: there's no chance / no way that (informal).","on_screen":"certain: is bound to • is sure to • it's a foregone conclusion that • probable: there's every chance that • in all likelihood • the odds are that • stand a good chance of + -ing • possible: might conceivably • could potentially • there's a chance that","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Technology is bound to change the way we work. There's every chance that people will live longer than ever before. In all likelihood, the first crewed Mars landing will take place in the 2030s or later.","on_screen":"Technology is bound to change the way we work. / There's every chance that people will live longer than ever before. / In all likelihood, the first crewed Mars landing will take place in the 2030s or later.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “There's every chance to people live longer.” That’s not right. We say: “There's every chance that people will live longer.” every chance + that-clause.","on_screen":"✗ There's every chance to people live longer.   ✓ There's every chance that people will live longer.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Which is more certain: \"is bound to\" or \"could potentially\"? … Think! … *is bound to*. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"There's every chance that people will live longer.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"There's every chance that people will live longer.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","اليقين: is bound to / is sure to / it's a foregone conclusion that.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: There's every chance to people live longer. — الصحيح: There's every chance that people will live longer.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول There's every chance that people will live longer."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Technology is ___ to change the way we work; there is no doubt about it.","options":["every chance","conceivably","bound","doubtful"],"answer":"bound","explanation":"Certain: bound to, sure to, foregone conclusion.","explanation_ar":"اليقين."},{"q":"There's every chance ___ people will live longer than ever before.","options":["that","for","of","to"],"answer":"that","explanation":"Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"Our team stands a good chance of ___ the robotics competition.","options":["winning","won","win","to win"],"answer":"winning","explanation":"Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"___, the first crewed Mars landing will take place in the 2030s or later.","options":["Bound to","Every chance","The odds against","In all likelihood"],"answer":"In all likelihood","explanation":"Probable: every chance, in all likelihood, the odds are.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال الكبير."},{"q":"There's ___ chance that humans will live on Mars by 2030; most experts think it will take much longer.","options":["a good","a safe","every","little"],"answer":"little","explanation":"Unlikely / impossible: little chance, doubtful, odds against.","explanation_ar":"عدم الترجيح."},{"q":"Although some engineers are optimistic, most transport experts consider it ______ that flying taxis will become a common sight in large cities within the next five years.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["inevitable","doubtful","bound","a foregone conclusion"],"answer":"doubtful","explanation":"Choose the right degree.","explanation_ar":"درجة الاحتمال."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite with the expression in brackets.","items":[{"q":"It is very probable that the population will peak this century. (In all likelihood)  (probability)","options":[],"answer":"In all likelihood, the population will peak this century.","explanation":"Probable: every chance, in all likelihood, the odds are.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال الكبير."},{"q":"It is unlikely that humans will live on Mars by 2030. (The odds are against)  (probability)","options":[],"answer":"The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2030.","explanation":"Unlikely / impossible: little chance, doubtful, odds against.","explanation_ar":"عدم الترجيح."},{"q":"It is certain that AI will change education. (is bound to)  (probability)","options":[],"answer":"AI is bound to change education.","explanation":"Certain: bound to, sure to, foregone conclusion.","explanation_ar":"اليقين."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"She stands a good chance to win the scholarship.","options":[],"answer":"She stands a good chance of winning the scholarship.","explanation":"to win → of winning. Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: to win → of winning. القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"There's every chance to people live longer.","options":[],"answer":"There's every chance that people will live longer.","explanation":"to people live → that people will live. Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: to people live → that people will live. القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"Technology is bound changing our lives.","options":[],"answer":"Technology is bound to change our lives.","explanation":"changing → to change. Certain: bound to, sure to, foregone conclusion.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: changing → to change. اليقين."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Rate future possibilities.","items":[{"q":"Rate \"robots will teach in classrooms by 2040\".","options":[],"answer":"Robots could potentially help teachers, but there's little chance that they will replace them.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rate \"people will live to 120\".","options":[],"answer":"It's doubtful whether most people will live to 120, but longevity is bound to increase.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rate \"Saudi astronauts will visit the Moon\".","options":[],"answer":"There's every chance that Saudi astronauts will visit the Moon one day.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"It's a safe bet that…\".","options":[],"answer":"It's a safe bet that smartphones will look very different in twenty years.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"stand a good chance of + -ing\".","options":[],"answer":"Students who read widely stand a good chance of doing well at university.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A forecast for your city.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 forecasts for your city in 2050 with three different degrees of probability.","options":[],"answer":"Riyadh is bound to grow larger. In all likelihood, most cars will be electric. There's a slim chance that the city will have flying taxis.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"It's a foregone conclusion that…\".","options":[],"answer":"It's a foregone conclusion that technology will keep changing faster.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one question with \"What are the chances that…?\".","options":[],"answer":"What are the chances that the desert will become green again?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Tic-Tac-Grammar","mechanic":"tic_tac_toe","rules":["Two teams: X and O. Team X chooses a square.","Using the cue, the team says a correct sentence with probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) (the form in brackets: +, – or ?).","If the class and teacher agree it is correct, the team marks the square.","Three squares in a row wins. Play a second round with the grid turned around."],"data":[{"cue":"the population / reach / ten billion / by 2050 (future perfect)"},{"cue":"astronauts / live / in space / for fifty years / by 2050 (future perfect continuous)"},{"cue":"in 1900 / the writer / predict / people / be able to call without wires"},{"cue":"experts / believe / by 2000 / humans / build / Moon cities (would have)"},{"cue":"the settlement / be to / construct / decades ago (was to have been)"},{"cue":"there / every chance / people / live longer"},{"cue":"in all likelihood / the base / be decades away"},{"cue":"the odds / be / that robots / do many jobs"},{"cue":"young people / be bound / shape the future"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"With so many new hotels opening, tourism is ___ to grow.","options":["conceivably","every chance","sure","doubtful"],"answer":"sure","explanation":"Certain: bound to, sure to, foregone conclusion.","explanation_ar":"اليقين."},{"q":"___, the new law will be approved next month.","options":["Every chance to","In all probability","Bound to","Odds against"],"answer":"In all probability","explanation":"Probable: every chance, in all likelihood, the odds are.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال الكبير."},{"q":"The odds are ___ the team winning without its best player.","options":["of","that","to","against"],"answer":"against","explanation":"Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"Robots ___ replace some routine office jobs, but nobody is sure.","options":["could potentially","it's a foregone conclusion","are bound to","there's every chance"],"answer":"could potentially","explanation":"Possible: could potentially, might conceivably.","explanation_ar":"الإمكان."},{"q":"It's ___ whether flying cars will ever be common.","options":["sure","a safe bet","doubtful","bound"],"answer":"doubtful","explanation":"Unlikely / impossible: little chance, doubtful, odds against.","explanation_ar":"عدم الترجيح."},{"q":"","options":["It's a safe bet of electric cars becoming cheaper.","It's a safe bet that electric cars will become cheaper.","It's a safe bet to electric cars become cheaper.","It's safe bet that electric cars will become cheaper."],"answer":"It's a safe bet that electric cars will become cheaper.","explanation":"Probable: every chance, in all likelihood, the odds are.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال الكبير."},{"q":"","options":["There's no chance to finishing the project today.","There's no chances that we'll finish the project today.","There's no chance that we'll finish the project today.","There's no chance of we finish the project today."],"answer":"There's no chance that we'll finish the project today.","explanation":"Unlikely / impossible: little chance, doubtful, odds against.","explanation_ar":"عدم الترجيح."},{"q":"Given the team's strong record and the experience of its members, the judges agreed that the young engineers ______ winning the national innovation prize.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["stood a good chance of","had every chance that","stood a good chance to","were bound of"],"answer":"stood a good chance of","explanation":"Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"Because the outcome of the vote had been clear for weeks, the result was considered ______ long before the final count was announced.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["potentially possible","a slim chance","doubtful","a foregone conclusion"],"answer":"a foregone conclusion","explanation":"Choose the right degree.","explanation_ar":"درجة الاحتمال."},{"q":"Which expression shows the lowest probability?","options":["There's a slim chance that…","There's every chance that…","It's a safe bet that…","In all likelihood…"],"answer":"There's a slim chance that…","explanation":"Choose the right degree.","explanation_ar":"درجة الاحتمال."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A futures conference: a speaker in front of a \"probability scale\" from \"No chance\" to \"Bound to happen\"; cards placed along it: \"flying taxis\", \"Mars colony\", \"robot teachers\", \"10 billion people\"; audience members voting with colored cards. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A futures conference: a speaker in front of a \"probability scale\" from \"No chance\" to \"Bound to happen\"; cards placed along it: \"flying taxis\", \"Mars colony\", \"robot teachers\", \"10 billion people\"; audience members voting with colored cards"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “The Future of Humanity”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt)"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows speculative (based on guesses rather than facts); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing speculative: based on guesses rather than facts"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows extrapolate (to estimate future results by extending present trends); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing extrapolate: to estimate future results by extending present trends"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows inevitable (certain to happen and impossible to avoid); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing inevitable: certain to happen and impossible to avoid"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows utopia (an imagined perfect society); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing utopia: an imagined perfect society"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Tic-Tac-Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Tic-Tac-Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W01","script":"speculative. speculative. His ideas about time travel are highly speculative.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W02","script":"extrapolate. extrapolate. Scientists extrapolate from current data to predict temperatures.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W03","script":"inevitable. inevitable. Change is inevitable.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-W04","script":"utopia. utopia. Some writers imagine a utopia without poverty.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “There's Every Chance; In All Likelihood: Expressing Degrees of Probability”. Let’s learn probability expressions from certain to impossible: certain (is bound to, is sure to, it's a foregone conclusion that, without a doubt); very probable (there's every chance that, in all likelihood, the chances / odds are that, it's a safe bet that, stand a good chance of + -ing); possible (might conceivably, could potentially, there's a chance that); unlikely (there's little / a slim chance that, it's doubtful whether, the odds are against); impossible (there's no chance / no way that); adverbs (undoubtedly, presumably, conceivably, in all probability) together. Remember these patterns: In all likelihood, the population will peak this century. There's little chance that humans will live on Mars by 2030. What are the chances that robots will replace teachers? Certain or almost certain: is bound to / is sure to + verb (Technology is bound to change the way we work.); it's a foregone conclusion that (the result is already known); undoubtedly. Very probable: there's every chance that (People will live longer – there's every chance.); in all likelihood / in all probability (In all likelihood, the first Mars landing will be in the 2030s.); the chances / odds are that; it's a safe bet that; stand a good chance of + -ing (She stands a good chance of winning). Possible: might conceivably / could potentially (Robots could potentially replace some teachers.); there's a chance that. Unlikely: there's little / a slim chance that; it's doubtful whether / that; the odds are against + noun / -ing (The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2030.); presumably means \"I assume, but I'm not certain\". Impossible: there's no chance / no way that (informal). Here are some examples. Technology is bound to change the way we work. There's every chance that people will live longer than ever before. In all likelihood, the first crewed Mars landing will take place in the 2030s or later. Be careful! Some learners say: “There's every chance to people live longer.” That’s not right. We say: “There's every chance that people will live longer.” every chance + that-clause. Quick check: Which is more certain: \"is bound to\" or \"could potentially\"? … Think! … *is bound to*. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"There's every chance that people will live longer.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3","title":"Utopia, Dystopia, Trajectory: The Vocabulary of Futures Studies","objectives":["Students will be able to discuss possible futures with precise vocabulary and collocations.","Students will be able to use word parts (fore-, dys-, extra-) to understand new words.","I can describe a worst-case scenario and a contingency plan.","I can explain the difference between a utopia and a dystopia."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من مناقشة المستقبلات المحتملة بمفردات ومتلازمات دقيقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام أجزاء الكلمات (fore- / dys- / extra-) لفهم كلمات جديدة.","أستطيع أن أصف أسوأ سيناريو وخطة طوارئ.","أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين المدينة الفاضلة والمدينة الفاسدة."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"dystopia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈstoʊpiə/","definition":"an imagined society in which life is very bad","arabic":"المدينة الفاسدة / عالم ديستوبي","example":"The novel describes a dystopia controlled by machines.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W01"},{"word":"longevity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/lɔnˈdʒɛvəti/","definition":"long life","arabic":"طول العمر","example":"Diet and exercise contribute to longevity.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W02"},{"word":"colonize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈkɑləˌnaɪz/","definition":"to send people to live in a new place and control it","arabic":"يستعمر / يستوطن","example":"Some scientists hope to colonize Mars.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W03"},{"word":"existential","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɛɡˌzɪˈstɛnʃəl/","definition":"connected with the existence or survival of something","arabic":"وجودي","example":"Climate change is an existential threat.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W04"},{"word":"contingency","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈtɪndʒənsi/","definition":"a possible future event that you must plan for","arabic":"طارئ / احتمال","example":"The team prepared a contingency plan.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W05"},{"word":"paradigm","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpɛrəˌdaɪm/","definition":"a model or set of ideas that shapes how people think","arabic":"نموذج فكري / براديغم","example":"The internet created a new paradigm for communication.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W06"},{"word":"foresee","pos":"verb","ipa":"/fɔrˈsi/","definition":"to know or expect that something will happen","arabic":"يتوقّع / يستشرف","example":"Few people foresaw the rise of smartphones.","image_id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Predicting: a forecast (a prediction based on information), a projection (an estimate based on current trends), to extrapolate (extend present trends into the future), a trajectory (the path of development), to foresee (know in advance), speculative (based on guesses). Planning: a scenario (a possible sequence of events), a contingency plan (for unexpected events), a paradigm shift (a change in the basic way of thinking).\nImagined societies: a utopia (a perfect society) and a dystopia (a terrible society, often controlled by technology or power) – popular genres in literature and film. Big questions: longevity (long life), colonize (settle another planet), an existential threat (a danger to survival), inevitable (impossible to avoid).\nWord parts: fore- = before (foresee, forecast, foresight, foreshadow); extra- = beyond (extrapolate, extraordinary, extraterrestrial); dys- = bad (dystopia, dysfunction); u- / eu- (Greek \"no / good\" place: utopia). Collocations: a worst-case / best-case scenario; a population projection; draw up a contingency plan; a paradigm shift; an existential threat; foresee the consequences; extrapolate from current data.\nCollocations/phrases: a worst-case scenario • a population projection • a long-term forecast • an upward trajectory | draw up a contingency plan • a paradigm shift • an existential threat • foresee the consequences | extrapolate from current data • highly speculative • virtually inevitable • increase longevity | fore- = before • extra- = beyond • dys- = bad • a dystopian novel","examples":["The forecast predicts heavy rain this weekend.","UN projections suggest the population will peak this century.","In the worst-case scenario, sea levels could rise by a meter.","The company drew up a contingency plan in case of a power cut.","The internet caused a paradigm shift in communication.","Some scientists consider climate change an existential threat.","The novel describes a dystopia controlled by machines.","Few people foresaw how quickly smartphones would spread."],"common_errors":["✗ The weather forecasting says it will rain. → ✓ The weather forecast says it will rain. (Noun → forecast.)","✗ Change is inevitably. → ✓ Change is inevitable. (Adjective → inevitable.)","✗ The film shows a dystopia where everyone is happy and free. → ✓ The film shows a utopia where everyone is happy and free. (Perfect society → utopia.)","✗ Nobody could foreseen the crisis. → ✓ Nobody could have foreseen the crisis. / Nobody could foresee the crisis. (foresee – foresaw – foreseen.)"],"arabic":"كلمات التنبؤ: forecast (توقع) وprojection (إسقاط) وextrapolate (يستقرئ) وtrajectory (مسار) وforesee (يستشرف) وspeculative (تخميني).\nكلمات التخطيط: scenario (سيناريو) وcontingency plan (خطة طوارئ) وparadigm shift (تحوّل في النموذج الفكري).\nutopia (المدينة الفاضلة) عكس dystopia (المدينة الفاسدة)، وexistential threat (تهديد وجودي)، وlongevity (طول العمر)، وinevitable (حتمي).\nأجزاء الكلمات: fore- = قبل (foresee)، وextra- = ما وراء (extrapolate)، وdys- = سيئ (dystopia)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Utopia, Dystopia, Trajectory: The Vocabulary of Futures Studies”. Let’s learn futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U4-LS3 | Utopia, Dystopia, Trajectory: The Vocabulary of Futures Studies","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. dystopia: an imagined society in which life is very bad. longevity: long life. colonize: to send people to live in a new place and control it.","on_screen":"dystopia | longevity | colonize","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Predicting: a forecast (a prediction based on information), a projection (an estimate based on current trends), to extrapolate (extend present trends into the future), a trajectory (the path of development), to foresee (know in advance), speculative (based on guesses). Planning: a scenario (a possible sequence of events), a contingency plan (for unexpected events), a paradigm shift (a change in the basic way of thinking). Imagined societies: a utopia (a perfect society) and a dystopia (a terrible society, often controlled by technology or power) – popular genres in literature and film. Big questions: longevity (long life), colonize (settle another planet), an existential threat (a danger to survival), inevitable (impossible to avoid).","on_screen":"futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The forecast predicts heavy rain this weekend. UN projections suggest the population will peak this century. In the worst-case scenario, sea levels could rise by a meter.","on_screen":"The forecast predicts heavy rain this weekend. / UN projections suggest the population will peak this century. / In the worst-case scenario, sea levels could rise by a meter.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The weather forecasting says it will rain.” That’s not right. We say: “The weather forecast says it will rain.” Noun → forecast.","on_screen":"✗ The weather forecasting says it will rain.   ✓ The weather forecast says it will rain.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: What does \"fore-\" mean in \"foresee\"? … Think! … Before. Great job! Now you can say: I can describe a worst-case scenario and a contingency plan.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can describe a worst-case scenario and a contingency plan.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","كلمات التنبؤ: forecast (توقع) وprojection (إسقاط) وextrapolate (يستقرئ) وtrajectory (مسار) وforesee (يستشرف) وspeculative (تخميني).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The weather forecasting says it will rain. — الصحيح: The weather forecast says it will rain.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أصف أسوأ سيناريو وخطة طوارئ."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"UN population ___ suggest that the world's population will peak this century.","options":["utopias","contingencies","projections","paradigms"],"answer":"projections","explanation":"Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"In the worst-case ___, sea levels could rise by a meter.","options":["longevity","trajectory","paradigm","scenario"],"answer":"scenario","explanation":"Futures collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"The company drew up a ___ plan in case of a power cut.","options":["trajectory","contingency","dystopian","speculative"],"answer":"contingency","explanation":"Futures collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"The novel describes a ___ in which machines control every human decision.","options":["longevity","forecast","dystopia","utopia"],"answer":"dystopia","explanation":"Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The prefix \"fore-\" in \"foresee\" and \"forecast\" means ___.","options":["against","bad","beyond","before"],"answer":"before","explanation":"Word parts (fore-, extra-, dys-).","explanation_ar":"أجزاء الكلمة."},{"q":"Because the researchers had data from only three years, their predictions for the next half-century were described as highly ______ and were treated with caution.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["speculative","inevitable","utopian","existential"],"answer":"speculative","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"extrapolate","options":["to estimate future results by extending present trends","a model of ideas that shapes how people think","the path of development over time","connected with survival or existence","long life","certain to happen and impossible to avoid"],"answer":"to estimate future results by extending present trends","explanation":"extrapolate → to estimate future results by extending present trends. Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"trajectory","options":["to estimate future results by extending present trends","a model of ideas that shapes how people think","the path of development over time","connected with survival or existence","long life","certain to happen and impossible to avoid"],"answer":"the path of development over time","explanation":"trajectory → the path of development over time. Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"paradigm","options":["to estimate future results by extending present trends","a model of ideas that shapes how people think","the path of development over time","connected with survival or existence","long life","certain to happen and impossible to avoid"],"answer":"a model of ideas that shapes how people think","explanation":"paradigm → a model of ideas that shapes how people think. Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"longevity","options":["to estimate future results by extending present trends","a model of ideas that shapes how people think","the path of development over time","connected with survival or existence","long life","certain to happen and impossible to avoid"],"answer":"long life","explanation":"longevity → long life. Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"inevitable","options":["to estimate future results by extending present trends","a model of ideas that shapes how people think","the path of development over time","connected with survival or existence","long life","certain to happen and impossible to avoid"],"answer":"certain to happen and impossible to avoid","explanation":"inevitable → certain to happen and impossible to avoid. Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"existential","options":["to estimate future results by extending present trends","a model of ideas that shapes how people think","the path of development over time","connected with survival or existence","long life","certain to happen and impossible to avoid"],"answer":"connected with survival or existence","explanation":"existential → connected with survival or existence. Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Imagine the future.","items":[{"q":"Describe a best-case scenario for your city in 2050.","options":[],"answer":"In the best-case scenario, Riyadh will be a green city with clean air and fast public transport.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a worst-case scenario for the oceans.","options":[],"answer":"In the worst-case scenario, coral reefs will disappear completely.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"What contingency plan does your family have for emergencies?","options":[],"answer":"We have a contingency plan: we keep water, food and a first-aid kit at home.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Name something nobody foresaw twenty years ago.","options":[],"answer":"Few people foresaw that we would all watch films on our phones.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Would you like to live in a utopia? Why or why not?","options":[],"answer":"A utopia sounds wonderful, but I think a perfect society might be boring.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A futures report.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about the future of your country using 3 futures words.","options":[],"answer":"Population projections suggest steady growth. The economic trajectory is positive. However, we need a contingency plan for water shortages.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences explaining a paradigm shift that you have seen.","options":[],"answer":"Online learning was a paradigm shift in education. Students no longer have to be in the same room as their teachers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"extrapolate from\".","options":[],"answer":"If we extrapolate from current trends, most cars will be electric by 2040.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Memory Match","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Place all cards face down in a grid.","Turn over two cards. If the word matches its definition, keep the pair and say a sentence with the word.","If not, turn them back over; the next player plays.","The player with the most pairs wins."],"data":[{"word":"forecast","match":"a statement about what is likely to happen, based on information","arabic":"توقّع / تنبؤ"},{"word":"projection","match":"an estimate of a future amount based on present trends","arabic":"إسقاط / تقدير مستقبلي"},{"word":"trajectory","match":"the path or direction of development over time","arabic":"مسار"},{"word":"scenario","match":"a possible situation or sequence of events in the future","arabic":"سيناريو"},{"word":"speculative","match":"based on guesses rather than facts","arabic":"تخميني"},{"word":"extrapolate","match":"to estimate future results by extending present trends","arabic":"يستقرئ / يمدّ الاتجاه"},{"word":"inevitable","match":"certain to happen and impossible to avoid","arabic":"حتمي"},{"word":"utopia","match":"an imagined perfect society","arabic":"المدينة الفاضلة"},{"word":"dystopia","match":"an imagined society in which life is very bad","arabic":"المدينة الفاسدة / عالم ديستوبي"},{"word":"longevity","match":"long life","arabic":"طول العمر"},{"word":"colonize","match":"to send people to live in a new place and control it","arabic":"يستعمر / يستوطن"},{"word":"existential","match":"connected with the existence or survival of something","arabic":"وجودي"},{"word":"contingency","match":"a possible future event that you must plan for","arabic":"طارئ / احتمال"},{"word":"paradigm","match":"a model or set of ideas that shapes how people think","arabic":"نموذج فكري / براديغم"},{"word":"foresee","match":"to know or expect that something will happen","arabic":"يتوقّع / يستشرف"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Few people ___ how quickly smartphones would spread.","options":["forecasted at","foresaw","colonized","extrapolated to"],"answer":"foresaw","explanation":"Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Scientists ___ from current data to predict future temperatures.","options":["extrapolate","colonize","forecast up","foresee to"],"answer":"extrapolate","explanation":"Futures collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Some scientists hope to ___ Mars within this century.","options":["forecast","foresee","colonize","extrapolate"],"answer":"colonize","explanation":"Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Healthy diets and better medicine have increased human ___.","options":["paradigm","trajectory","contingency","longevity"],"answer":"longevity","explanation":"Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The internet created a ___ shift in how people communicate.","options":["longevity","utopia","paradigm","trajectory"],"answer":"paradigm","explanation":"Futures collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Many experts consider a large asteroid impact to be an ___ threat to humanity.","options":["inevitable to","utopian","existential","speculative"],"answer":"existential","explanation":"Futures collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"In Thomas More's book, \"Utopia\" is an imaginary island with a perfect society; a story about a terrible society is called a ___.","options":["trajectory","dystopia","utopia","paradigm"],"answer":"dystopia","explanation":"Word parts (fore-, extra-, dys-).","explanation_ar":"أجزاء الكلمة."},{"q":"The prefix \"extra-\" in \"extraterrestrial\" means ___.","options":["beyond","again","before","bad"],"answer":"beyond","explanation":"Word parts (fore-, extra-, dys-).","explanation_ar":"أجزاء الكلمة."},{"q":"Analysts noted that the company's sales had grown every year for a decade, an upward ______ that they expected to continue as long as demand remained strong.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["dystopia","contingency","longevity","trajectory"],"answer":"trajectory","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"Although many people fear that robots will take most jobs, economists point out that such an outcome is not ______; governments and companies can still make choices that shape the future of work.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["inevitable","existential","utopian","speculative"],"answer":"inevitable","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A futures workshop: a large wall chart showing three diverging paths labeled \"Utopia\", \"Business as usual\" and \"Dystopia\"; a graph of population projections; students writing scenarios on sticky notes. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A futures workshop: a large wall chart showing three diverging paths labeled \"Utopia\", \"Business as usual\" and \"Dystopia\"; a graph of population projections; students writing scenarios on sticky notes"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows dystopia (an imagined society in which life is very bad); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing dystopia: an imagined society in which life is very bad"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows longevity (long life); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing longevity: long life"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows colonize (to send people to live in a new place and control it); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing colonize: to send people to live in a new place and control it"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows existential (connected with the existence or survival of something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing existential: connected with the existence or survival of something"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows contingency (a possible future event that you must plan for); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing contingency: a possible future event that you must plan for"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows paradigm (a model or set of ideas that shapes how people think); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing paradigm: a model or set of ideas that shapes how people think"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows foresee (to know or expect that something will happen); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing foresee: to know or expect that something will happen"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Memory Match”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Memory Match"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W01","script":"dystopia. dystopia. The novel describes a dystopia controlled by machines.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W02","script":"longevity. longevity. Diet and exercise contribute to longevity.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W03","script":"colonize. colonize. Some scientists hope to colonize Mars.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W04","script":"existential. existential. Climate change is an existential threat.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W05","script":"contingency. contingency. The team prepared a contingency plan.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W06","script":"paradigm. paradigm. The internet created a new paradigm for communication.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-W07","script":"foresee. foresee. Few people foresaw the rise of smartphones.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Utopia, Dystopia, Trajectory: The Vocabulary of Futures Studies”. Let’s learn futures studies vocabulary (forecast, projection, trajectory, scenario, speculative, extrapolate, inevitable, utopia, dystopia, longevity, colonize, existential, contingency, paradigm, foresee); collocations (a worst-case scenario, a population projection, a contingency plan, a paradigm shift, an existential threat, extrapolate from current trends, foresee the consequences); prefixes and word parts (fore- = before: foresee, forecast, foresight; extra- = beyond; dys- = bad; eu- / u- = good / no) together. Meet some new words. dystopia: an imagined society in which life is very bad. longevity: long life. colonize: to send people to live in a new place and control it. Predicting: a forecast (a prediction based on information), a projection (an estimate based on current trends), to extrapolate (extend present trends into the future), a trajectory (the path of development), to foresee (know in advance), speculative (based on guesses). Planning: a scenario (a possible sequence of events), a contingency plan (for unexpected events), a paradigm shift (a change in the basic way of thinking). Imagined societies: a utopia (a perfect society) and a dystopia (a terrible society, often controlled by technology or power) – popular genres in literature and film. Big questions: longevity (long life), colonize (settle another planet), an existential threat (a danger to survival), inevitable (impossible to avoid). Here are some examples. The forecast predicts heavy rain this weekend. UN projections suggest the population will peak this century. In the worst-case scenario, sea levels could rise by a meter. Be careful! Some learners say: “The weather forecasting says it will rain.” That’s not right. We say: “The weather forecast says it will rain.” Noun → forecast. Quick check: What does \"fore-\" mean in \"foresee\"? … Think! … Before. Great job! Now you can say: I can describe a worst-case scenario and a contingency plan.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4","title":"The Student Wants to Emphasize…: Advanced Rhetorical Synthesis (SAT)","objectives":["Students will be able to answer complex rhetorical synthesis questions by matching every part of the goal.","Students will be able to write synthesis sentences that combine several notes for a precise purpose.","I can choose the option that matches every part of a two-part goal.","I can combine four notes into one sentence that emphasizes a contrast between expectation and outcome."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من الإجابة عن أسئلة التركيب البلاغي المعقدة بمطابقة كل جزء من الهدف.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة جمل تركيبية تدمج عدة ملاحظات لغرض دقيق.","أستطيع أن أختار الخيار الذي يطابق كل جزء من هدف ثنائي.","أستطيع أن أدمج أربع ملاحظات في جملة واحدة تبرز التناقض بين التوقع والنتيجة."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"forecast","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfɔrˌkæst/","definition":"a statement about what is likely to happen, based on information","arabic":"توقّع / تنبؤ","example":"The weather forecast predicts rain.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"projection","pos":"noun","ipa":"/prəˈdʒɛkʃən/","definition":"an estimate of a future amount based on present trends","arabic":"إسقاط / تقدير مستقبلي","example":"Population projections suggest growth until the 2080s.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"trajectory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/trəˈdʒɛktɚi/","definition":"the path or direction of development over time","arabic":"مسار","example":"The country's economic trajectory looks positive.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"scenario","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sɪˈnɛrioʊ/","definition":"a possible situation or sequence of events in the future","arabic":"سيناريو","example":"In the worst-case scenario, sea levels will rise by a meter.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"speculative","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈspɛkjələtɪv/","definition":"based on guesses rather than facts","arabic":"تخميني","example":"His ideas about time travel are highly speculative.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"extrapolate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɛkˈstræpəˌleɪt/","definition":"to estimate future results by extending present trends","arabic":"يستقرئ / يمدّ الاتجاه","example":"Scientists extrapolate from current data to predict temperatures.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Advanced synthesis goals often have two parts: \"emphasize the aim of the study and its finding\"; \"present a prediction and explain why it failed\". The correct answer must do both. Many wrong options are true and relevant but only do one part – or add information the goal does not ask for.\nGoal types and signal words: contrast between expectation and outcome (Although experts predicted…, in fact…); aim / method of a study (To find out whether…, researchers surveyed / measured…); limitation (However, the study included only…); generalization (like other…, many…); comparison of two predictions (While X predicted…, Y predicted…); specific example (For example / such as + a precise detail); audience familiar with the topic (no need to define basic terms – focus on the new information).\nStrategy: (1) underline every key word in the goal (both parts); (2) for each option, ask \"Does it do part 1? part 2?\"; (3) eliminate options that define something when the audience already knows it, or give background when the goal asks for a result; (4) choose the most complete and precise option. When writing, use subordination to fit several notes into one sentence: Although…, …; To…, researchers…, finding that….\ntwo-part goals → the answer must do both parts\nexpectation vs outcome → although / in fact • aim → to + verb • method → surveyed / measured\nlimitation → however, only… • generalization → like many… / most…\naudience familiar with the topic → don't define basic terms","examples":["Expectation vs outcome: Although experts in the 1960s predicted Moon cities by 2000, no permanent lunar base had been built by then.","Aim and method: To find out whether reading on paper improves memory, researchers tested 200 students with printed and digital texts.","Limitation: The study found a strong effect; however, it included only 40 participants.","Generalization: Like many early predictions about technology, the 1900 forecasts overestimated transport and underestimated communication.","Two predictions: While one model predicts a population peak in the 2080s, another predicts an earlier peak.","Specific example: Some 1900 predictions proved accurate, such as the forecast of wireless telephone calls.","Familiar audience: The 2023 mission included the first Saudi woman to travel into space.","Aim and finding: Seeking to measure the effect of sleep on memory, the researchers found that well-rested students remembered 20% more words."],"common_errors":["✗ Goal: present the aim and the finding. Answer: \"Researchers studied sleep.\" → ✓ \"To measure the effect of sleep on memory, researchers found that…\" (Both parts needed.)","✗ Goal: audience already familiar with the ISS. Answer: \"The ISS, a space station in orbit around Earth, …\" → ✓ Focus on the new information, not the definition. (Don't define for a familiar audience.)","✗ Goal: emphasize a limitation. Answer: \"The study's results were impressive.\" → ✓ \"However, the study included only forty participants.\" (Limitation → only / small / short.)"],"arabic":"أهداف التركيب المتقدمة غالبًا من جزأين، مثل «إبراز هدف الدراسة ونتيجتها»، والإجابة الصحيحة يجب أن تحقق الجزأين معًا.\nكثير من الخيارات الخاطئة صحيحة وذات صلة لكنها تحقق جزءًا واحدًا فقط أو تضيف معلومات لا يطلبها الهدف.\nإشارات الأهداف: التوقع والنتيجة (although… in fact)، والهدف والمنهج (to find out… researchers surveyed)، والقيد (however, only)، والتعميم (like many…).\nإذا كان الجمهور يعرف الموضوع فلا داعي لتعريف المصطلحات الأساسية، بل نركز على المعلومة الجديدة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Student Wants to Emphasize…: Advanced Rhetorical Synthesis (SAT)”. Let’s learn advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U4-LS4 | The Student Wants to Emphasize…: Advanced Rhetorical Synthesis (SAT)","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Advanced synthesis goals often have two parts: \"emphasize the aim of the study and its finding\"; \"present a prediction and explain why it failed\". The correct answer must do both. Many wrong options are true and relevant but only do one part – or add information the goal does not ask for.","on_screen":"advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Advanced synthesis goals often have two parts: \"emphasize the aim of the study and its finding\"; \"present a prediction and explain why it failed\". The correct answer must do both. Many wrong options are true and relevant but only do one part – or add information the goal does not ask for. Goal types and signal words: contrast between expectation and outcome (Although experts predicted…, in fact…); aim / method of a study (To find out whether…, researchers surveyed / measured…); limitation (However, the study included only…); generalization (like other…, many…); comparison of two predictions (While X predicted…, Y predicted…); specific example (For example / such as + a precise detail); audience familiar with the topic (no need to define basic terms – focus on the new information).","on_screen":"two-part goals → the answer must do both parts • expectation vs outcome → although / in fact • aim → to + verb • method → surveyed / measured • limitation → however, only… • generalization → like many… / most…","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Expectation vs outcome: Although experts in the 1960s predicted Moon cities by 2000, no permanent lunar base had been built by then. Aim and method: To find out whether reading on paper improves memory, researchers tested 200 students with printed and digital texts. Limitation: The study found a strong effect; however, it included only 40 participants.","on_screen":"Expectation vs outcome: Although experts in the 1960s predicted Moon cities by 2000, no permanent lunar base had been built by then. / Aim and method: To find out whether reading on paper improves memory, researchers tested 200 students with printed and digital texts. / Limitation: The study found a strong effect; however, it included only 40 participants.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Goal: present the aim and the finding. Answer: \"Researchers studied sleep.\".” That’s not right. We say: “\"To measure the effect of sleep on memory, researchers found that…\".” Both parts needed.","on_screen":"✗ Goal: present the aim and the finding. Answer: \"Researchers studied sleep.\"   ✓ \"To measure the effect of sleep on memory, researchers found that…\"","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: If the goal has two parts, how many must the answer achieve? … Think! … Both. Great job! Now you can say: I can choose the option that matches every part of a two-part goal.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can choose the option that matches every part of a two-part goal.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","أهداف التركيب المتقدمة غالبًا من جزأين، مثل «إبراز هدف الدراسة ونتيجتها»، والإجابة الصحيحة يجب أن تحقق الجزأين معًا.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Goal: present the aim and the finding. Answer: \"Researchers studied sleep.\" — الصحيح: \"To measure the effect of sleep on memory, researchers found that…\"","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أختار الخيار الذي يطابق كل جزء من هدف ثنائي."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the best answer.","items":[{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• In the 1960s, many experts predicted permanent bases on the Moon by 2000.\n• The last crewed Moon landing took place in 1972.\n• No permanent lunar base had been built by 2000.\n• Several countries now plan to return to the Moon.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize the contrast between the experts' expectations and what actually happened. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The last crewed Moon landing took place in 1972.","In the 1960s, experts made many predictions about space.","Although many experts in the 1960s predicted permanent Moon bases by 2000, no such base had been built by then.","Several countries now plan to return to the Moon."],"answer":"Although many experts in the 1960s predicted permanent Moon bases by 2000, no such base had been built by then.","explanation":"Expectation vs outcome.","explanation_ar":"التوقع والنتيجة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Researchers wanted to know whether reading on paper improves memory.\n• They tested 200 students.\n• Half read a printed text, and half read the same text on a screen.\n• Students who read on paper remembered more details.\n\nThe student wants to present the study's aim and its finding. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Seeking to learn whether reading on paper improves memory, researchers found that students who read printed texts remembered more details.","Researchers tested 200 students.","Students read the same text.","Half the students read on a screen."],"answer":"Seeking to learn whether reading on paper improves memory, researchers found that students who read printed texts remembered more details.","explanation":"Aim, method or finding of a study.","explanation_ar":"الهدف والمنهج والنتيجة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• A study found that a new learning app improved vocabulary scores by 30%.\n• The study lasted only two weeks.\n• It included 25 students from one school.\n• The researchers called for larger studies.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize a limitation of the study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The app improved vocabulary scores by 30%.","Although the app improved vocabulary scores by 30%, the study lasted only two weeks and included just 25 students from one school.","The study was about a learning app.","The researchers were happy with the results."],"answer":"Although the app improved vocabulary scores by 30%, the study lasted only two weeks and included just 25 students from one school.","explanation":"Limitation.","explanation_ar":"القيد."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• In 1900, a magazine predicted that people would make wireless phone calls.\n• The same article predicted that most city traffic would move below or above ground.\n• Wireless calls became common by the 2000s.\n• Most city traffic still moves at street level.\n\nThe student wants to show that the 1900 predictions were only partly accurate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Wireless calls became common by the 2000s.","In 1900, a magazine predicted wireless phone calls.","Magazines often make predictions.","The 1900 article correctly predicted wireless phone calls, but its forecast that most city traffic would move below or above ground proved wrong."],"answer":"The 1900 article correctly predicted wireless phone calls, but its forecast that most city traffic would move below or above ground proved wrong.","explanation":"Two-part goal.","explanation_ar":"الهدف الثنائي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• The International Space Station (ISS) has been continuously occupied since November 2000.\n• Astronauts from many countries have lived aboard it.\n• In 2023, Saudi astronauts Rayyanah Barnawi and Ali AlQarni visited the ISS.\n• Barnawi was the first Saudi woman to travel into space.\n\nThe student wants to present the Saudi mission to an audience already familiar with the ISS. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The ISS orbits the Earth.","Astronauts from many countries have lived aboard the ISS.","The ISS is a space station that has been continuously occupied since November 2000.","In 2023, Rayyanah Barnawi, the first Saudi woman in space, and Ali AlQarni visited the ISS."],"answer":"In 2023, Rayyanah Barnawi, the first Saudi woman in space, and Ali AlQarni visited the ISS.","explanation":"Audience familiar / unfamiliar with the topic.","explanation_ar":"الجمهور."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• One UN projection suggests that the world population will peak at about 10.3 billion in the 2080s.\n• Some other researchers predict an earlier peak.\n• They argue that birth rates are falling faster than expected.\n• Both groups agree that growth is slowing.\n\nThe student wants to compare the two predictions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The UN makes population projections.","Birth rates are falling.","While one UN projection suggests a population peak of about 10.3 billion in the 2080s, other researchers predict an earlier peak because birth rates are falling faster than expected.","Both groups agree that growth is slowing."],"answer":"While one UN projection suggests a population peak of about 10.3 billion in the 2080s, other researchers predict an earlier peak because birth rates are falling faster than expected.","explanation":"Generalization or comparison.","explanation_ar":"التعميم والمقارنة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-B1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Guided practice: True or False? Does the sentence fully accomplish the goal?","items":[{"q":"Goal: present a study's aim and finding. Sentence: \"Researchers studied the effects of music on concentration.\"","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “the text”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Goal: emphasize a limitation. Sentence: \"However, the survey included only students from a single city.\"","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “the text”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Goal: introduce a well-known scientist to an audience that already knows her. Sentence: \"Marie Curie, a scientist, was a person who studied science.\"","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “the text”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine the notes into one sentence that meets the goal.","items":[{"q":"Experts predicted flying cars by 2000.  +  Flying cars are still rare today. (Goal: contrast expectation and outcome – although)","options":[],"answer":"Although experts predicted flying cars by 2000, they are still rare today.","explanation":"Expectation vs outcome.","explanation_ar":"التوقع والنتيجة."},{"q":"Researchers wanted to know if exercise improves mood.  +  They found that a 20-minute walk improved mood. (Goal: aim and finding – to find out whether)","options":[],"answer":"To find out whether exercise improves mood, researchers studied walking and found that a 20-minute walk improved it.","explanation":"Aim, method or finding of a study.","explanation_ar":"الهدف والمنهج والنتيجة."},{"q":"The results were promising.  +  The study included only 30 people. (Goal: emphasize a limitation – however)","options":[],"answer":"The results were promising; however, the study included only 30 people.","explanation":"Limitation.","explanation_ar":"القيد."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write synthesis sentences for each goal.","items":[{"q":"Contrast an expectation and an outcome in your life.","options":[],"answer":"Although I expected the exam to be difficult, I finished it in half the time.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Present the aim and finding of an imaginary study about sleep.","options":[],"answer":"To find out how sleep affects grades, researchers surveyed 500 students and found that those who slept eight hours scored higher.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Emphasize a limitation of the study above.","options":[],"answer":"However, the survey relied on students' own reports of their sleep.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Make a generalization from an example.","options":[],"answer":"Like many new technologies, electric cars were expensive at first but became cheaper over time.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Compare two predictions about the future of work.","options":[],"answer":"While some experts predict that AI will eliminate many jobs, others believe it will create more jobs than it destroys.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Create an advanced synthesis question.","items":[{"q":"Write 4 notes about a prediction or a study.","options":[],"answer":"A 2015 report predicted that 50% of shopping would be online by 2025. • Online shopping grew quickly. • By 2025, it was still well below 50% in most countries. • Many shoppers still prefer physical stores.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a two-part goal for your notes.","options":[],"answer":"The student wants to present the prediction and show that it was not fully accurate.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write the answer that fully meets your two-part goal.","options":[],"answer":"A 2015 report predicted that half of all shopping would be online by 2025, but in most countries the share remained well below that level.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Builder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The teacher reads a word and a short clue.","Teams build the word with letter tiles in 30 seconds.","The team holds up the word; correct spelling = 1 point, with correct capital letter or punctuation where needed = 1 bonus point.","Continue until all words are used."],"data":[{"word":"forecast","clue":"a statement about what is likely to happen, based on information"},{"word":"projection","clue":"an estimate of a future amount based on present trends"},{"word":"trajectory","clue":"the path or direction of development over time"},{"word":"scenario","clue":"a possible situation or sequence of events in the future"},{"word":"extrapolate","clue":"to estimate future results by extending present trends"},{"word":"contingency","clue":"a possible future event that you must plan for"},{"word":"speculative","clue":"based on guesses rather than facts"},{"word":"inevitable","clue":"certain to happen and impossible to avoid"},{"word":"utopia","clue":"an imagined perfect society"},{"word":"dystopia","clue":"an imagined society in which life is very bad"},{"word":"longevity","clue":"long life"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Scientists in the 1970s predicted that robots would do most housework by 2000.\n• Robot vacuum cleaners became popular in the 2000s.\n• Most other household tasks are still done by people.\n• Engineers say that tasks like folding laundry are very difficult for robots.\n\nThe student wants to explain why the 1970s prediction was only partly fulfilled. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Although robot vacuum cleaners became popular, most other household tasks are still done by people because tasks like folding laundry are very difficult for robots.","Robot vacuum cleaners became popular in the 2000s.","Scientists in the 1970s made predictions.","Engineers build robots."],"answer":"Although robot vacuum cleaners became popular, most other household tasks are still done by people because tasks like folding laundry are very difficult for robots.","explanation":"Two-part goal.","explanation_ar":"الهدف الثنائي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• A team of researchers studied how teenagers use their phones at night.\n• They tracked 300 teenagers for six months.\n• Teenagers who used phones after midnight slept 90 minutes less on average.\n• Their school grades were also lower.\n\nThe student wants to present the study's method. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Teenagers use phones.","The researchers tracked the nighttime phone use of 300 teenagers for six months.","Their grades were lower.","Teenagers who used phones after midnight slept less."],"answer":"The researchers tracked the nighttime phone use of 300 teenagers for six months.","explanation":"Aim, method or finding of a study.","explanation_ar":"الهدف والمنهج والنتيجة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Climate models predict that summers in the Gulf will become hotter.\n• One model predicts an average rise of 2°C by 2070.\n• The models are based on current emissions.\n• If emissions fall, the rise could be smaller.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize that the prediction depends on future choices. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["One model predicts a rise of 2°C by 2070.","Summers in the Gulf are hot.","Climate models are complex.","Because the models are based on current emissions, the predicted 2°C rise by 2070 could be smaller if emissions fall."],"answer":"Because the models are based on current emissions, the predicted 2°C rise by 2070 could be smaller if emissions fall.","explanation":"Limitation.","explanation_ar":"القيد."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Early cars were expensive and unreliable.\n• Within thirty years, they became affordable for many families.\n• Early mobile phones were also expensive and heavy.\n• Within twenty years, most people owned one.\n\nThe student wants to make a generalization about new technologies. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Like cars before them, mobile phones began as expensive luxuries but became affordable for most people within a few decades.","Many people own phones.","Mobile phones were heavy.","Early cars were unreliable."],"answer":"Like cars before them, mobile phones began as expensive luxuries but became affordable for most people within a few decades.","explanation":"Generalization or comparison.","explanation_ar":"التعميم والمقارنة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Many people expected e-books to replace printed books.\n• E-book sales grew rapidly after 2007.\n• Printed books still outsell e-books in many countries.\n• Some readers say they remember more from paper.\n\nThe student wants to contrast the expectation with the outcome. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Many people read books.","Some readers prefer paper.","E-book sales grew rapidly after 2007.","Although many expected e-books to replace printed books, printed books still outsell e-books in many countries."],"answer":"Although many expected e-books to replace printed books, printed books still outsell e-books in many countries.","explanation":"Expectation vs outcome.","explanation_ar":"التوقع والنتيجة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Vertical farms grow crops indoors in stacked layers.\n• They use up to 95% less water than traditional farms.\n• They are expensive to build and use a lot of electricity.\n• Several vertical farms have opened in the Gulf.\n\nThe student wants to present both an advantage and a disadvantage of vertical farms to an audience already familiar with them. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Several vertical farms have opened in the Gulf.","Vertical farms grow crops indoors in stacked layers.","Farming is important.","Vertical farms can use up to 95% less water than traditional farms, but they are expensive to build and use a lot of electricity."],"answer":"Vertical farms can use up to 95% less water than traditional farms, but they are expensive to build and use a lot of electricity.","explanation":"Audience familiar / unfamiliar with the topic.","explanation_ar":"الجمهور."},{"q":"A goal asks you to \"present the study and its main finding.\" An option describes only the study's method. Is it correct?","options":["Yes – the method is part of a study.","No – findings are never needed.","No – it achieves only one part of the goal.","Yes, if it is the longest option."],"answer":"No – it achieves only one part of the goal.","explanation":"Two-part goal.","explanation_ar":"الهدف الثنائي."},{"q":"Which signal phrase best introduces a limitation?","options":["Like many other studies, …","Although experts expected…","However, the study included only…","To find out whether…"],"answer":"However, the study included only…","explanation":"Limitation.","explanation_ar":"القيد."},{"q":"The goal says the audience is already familiar with artificial intelligence. Which option is LEAST appropriate?","options":["New AI tools can now diagnose some eye diseases.","Artificial intelligence, the ability of computers to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, …","AI translation has improved rapidly.","A 2024 AI model passed several medical exams."],"answer":"Artificial intelligence, the ability of computers to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, …","explanation":"Audience familiar / unfamiliar with the topic.","explanation_ar":"الجمهور."},{"q":"Which sentence emphasizes a contrast between expectation and outcome?","options":["The team won the championship.","Although the team expected to lose, it won the championship.","Like other teams, it trained hard.","The team practiced every day."],"answer":"Although the team expected to lose, it won the championship.","explanation":"Expectation vs outcome.","explanation_ar":"التوقع والنتيجة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “The Future of Humanity” that shows advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “The Future of Humanity” that shows advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence in action"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Builder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Builder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Student Wants to Emphasize…: Advanced Rhetorical Synthesis (SAT)”. Let’s learn advanced rhetorical synthesis: goals such as emphasizing a contrast between expectation and outcome, presenting a study's aim or method, explaining a limitation, making a generalization, comparing two predictions, supporting a claim with a specific example, introducing a source to an audience familiar with the topic; rejecting options that are true and relevant but only partly meet the goal; combining several notes into one precise sentence together. Advanced synthesis goals often have two parts: \"emphasize the aim of the study and its finding\"; \"present a prediction and explain why it failed\". The correct answer must do both. Many wrong options are true and relevant but only do one part – or add information the goal does not ask for. Advanced synthesis goals often have two parts: \"emphasize the aim of the study and its finding\"; \"present a prediction and explain why it failed\". The correct answer must do both. Many wrong options are true and relevant but only do one part – or add information the goal does not ask for. Goal types and signal words: contrast between expectation and outcome (Although experts predicted…, in fact…); aim / method of a study (To find out whether…, researchers surveyed / measured…); limitation (However, the study included only…); generalization (like other…, many…); comparison of two predictions (While X predicted…, Y predicted…); specific example (For example / such as + a precise detail); audience familiar with the topic (no need to define basic terms – focus on the new information). Here are some examples. Expectation vs outcome: Although experts in the 1960s predicted Moon cities by 2000, no permanent lunar base had been built by then. Aim and method: To find out whether reading on paper improves memory, researchers tested 200 students with printed and digital texts. Limitation: The study found a strong effect; however, it included only 40 participants. Be careful! Some learners say: “Goal: present the aim and the finding. Answer: \"Researchers studied sleep.\".” That’s not right. We say: “\"To measure the effect of sleep on memory, researchers found that…\".” Both parts needed. Quick check: If the goal has two parts, how many must the answer achieve? … Think! … Both. Great job! Now you can say: I can choose the option that matches every part of a two-part goal.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5","title":"My Vision for 2050: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to present a reasoned vision of the future.","Students will be able to write a balanced speculative essay with future forms, precise probability language and futures vocabulary, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can present my vision of the world in 2050.","I can write a balanced essay about the future."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من عرض رؤية مدروسة للمستقبل.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة مقال استشرافي متوازن بصيغ المستقبل ولغة احتمال دقيقة ومفردات دراسات المستقبل، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أعرض رؤيتي للعالم في عام 2050.","أستطيع أن أكتب مقالًا متوازنًا عن المستقبل."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"forecast","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfɔrˌkæst/","definition":"a statement about what is likely to happen, based on information","arabic":"توقّع / تنبؤ","example":"The weather forecast predicts rain.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"projection","pos":"noun","ipa":"/prəˈdʒɛkʃən/","definition":"an estimate of a future amount based on present trends","arabic":"إسقاط / تقدير مستقبلي","example":"Population projections suggest growth until the 2080s.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"trajectory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/trəˈdʒɛktɚi/","definition":"the path or direction of development over time","arabic":"مسار","example":"The country's economic trajectory looks positive.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"scenario","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sɪˈnɛrioʊ/","definition":"a possible situation or sequence of events in the future","arabic":"سيناريو","example":"In the worst-case scenario, sea levels will rise by a meter.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"speculative","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈspɛkjələtɪv/","definition":"based on guesses rather than facts","arabic":"تخميني","example":"His ideas about time travel are highly speculative.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"extrapolate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɛkˈstræpəˌleɪt/","definition":"to estimate future results by extending present trends","arabic":"يستقرئ / يمدّ الاتجاه","example":"Scientists extrapolate from current data to predict temperatures.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"inevitable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪˈnɛvətəbəl/","definition":"certain to happen and impossible to avoid","arabic":"حتمي","example":"Change is inevitable.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"utopia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/juˈtoʊpiə/","definition":"an imagined perfect society","arabic":"المدينة الفاضلة","example":"Some writers imagine a utopia without poverty.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"dystopia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈstoʊpiə/","definition":"an imagined society in which life is very bad","arabic":"المدينة الفاسدة / عالم ديستوبي","example":"The novel describes a dystopia controlled by machines.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"longevity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/lɔnˈdʒɛvəti/","definition":"long life","arabic":"طول العمر","example":"Diet and exercise contribute to longevity.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"colonize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈkɑləˌnaɪz/","definition":"to send people to live in a new place and control it","arabic":"يستعمر / يستوطن","example":"Some scientists hope to colonize Mars.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"existential","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɛɡˌzɪˈstɛnʃəl/","definition":"connected with the existence or survival of something","arabic":"وجودي","example":"Climate change is an existential threat.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"contingency","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈtɪndʒənsi/","definition":"a possible future event that you must plan for","arabic":"طارئ / احتمال","example":"The team prepared a contingency plan.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"paradigm","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpɛrəˌdaɪm/","definition":"a model or set of ideas that shapes how people think","arabic":"نموذج فكري / براديغم","example":"The internet created a new paradigm for communication.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"foresee","pos":"verb","ipa":"/fɔrˈsi/","definition":"to know or expect that something will happen","arabic":"يتوقّع / يستشرف","example":"Few people foresaw the rise of smartphones.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 4: the future perfect and future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary and advanced rhetorical synthesis.\nA balanced speculative essay begins with a hook (often a past prediction), separates likely from unlikely developments using precise probability language, considers both opportunities and risks, and ends by reminding the reader that the future depends on choices. Transitions such as even so, on the other hand and whatever happens guide the argument.\n✔ will have switched • will have been using • would be living (past prediction)\n✔ bound to • in all likelihood • a good chance • little chance\n✔ forecast, longevity, colonize, inevitable, scenario\n✔ expectation vs outcome: …believed that… That prediction failed…","examples":["In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon.","By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy.","Doctors will have been using AI for decades.","Electric vehicles are bound to dominate our roads.","In all likelihood, people will live longer.","There is little chance that humans will have colonized Mars.","Even so, some trends make certain outcomes probable.","The future is not inevitable."],"common_errors":["✗ By 2050, most countries will switch to renewable energy already. → ✓ By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy. (Future perfect.)","✗ In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we will live on the Moon. → ✓ In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon. (Future in the past.)","✗ There is little chance to humans colonize Mars. → ✓ There is little chance that humans will colonize Mars. (that-clause.)"],"arabic":"نراجع المستقبل التام والمستقبل في الماضي ودرجات الاحتمال ومفردات دراسات المستقبل والتركيب البلاغي المتقدم.\nالمقال الاستشرافي المتوازن يبدأ بمدخل (غالبًا تنبؤ قديم)، ويميز بين المرجح وغير المرجح بلغة احتمال دقيقة، ويوازن بين الفرص والمخاطر، وينتهي بأن المستقبل يعتمد على خياراتنا.\nالروابط مثل even so وon the other hand وwhatever happens ترشد الحجة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “My Vision for 2050: Language in Use”. Let’s learn future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U4-LS5 | My Vision for 2050: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Futures fair: prepare a 2-minute presentation of your vision for 2050 in one area (health, cities, space, education, work). Include two future perfect sentences, one past prediction that failed (would have…), three probability expressions of different strengths and three futures words. The audience rates each prediction on a probability scale.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 4: the future perfect and future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary and advanced rhetorical synthesis. A balanced speculative essay begins with a hook (often a past prediction), separates likely from unlikely developments using precise probability language, considers both opportunities and risks, and ends by reminding the reader that the future depends on choices. Transitions such as even so, on the other hand and whatever happens guide the argument.","on_screen":"✔ will have switched • will have been using • would be living (past prediction) • ✔ bound to • in all likelihood • a good chance • little chance • ✔ forecast, longevity, colonize, inevitable, scenario","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon. By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy. Doctors will have been using AI for decades.","on_screen":"In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon. / By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy. / Doctors will have been using AI for decades.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “By 2050, most countries will switch to renewable energy already.” That’s not right. We say: “By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy.” Future perfect.","on_screen":"✗ By 2050, most countries will switch to renewable energy already.   ✓ By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we will live on the Moon.”? … Think! … It should be “In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present my vision of the world in 2050.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can present my vision of the world in 2050.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع المستقبل التام والمستقبل في الماضي ودرجات الاحتمال ومفردات دراسات المستقبل والتركيب البلاغي المتقدم.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: By 2050, most countries will switch to renewable energy already. — الصحيح: By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أعرض رؤيتي للعالم في عام 2050."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"My Vision for 2050 – Rakan","body":"In 1970, many people believed that by 2000 we would be living in cities on the Moon.\nThat prediction failed, which reminds us that forecasting the future is never easy.\nEven so, some trends make certain outcomes highly probable.\nBy 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy, and electric vehicles are bound to dominate our roads.\nIn all likelihood, doctors will have been using AI to diagnose diseases for decades.\nThere is a good chance that people will live longer, but longevity will bring new challenges for health systems.\nOn the other hand, there is little chance that humans will have colonized Mars by then.\nWhatever happens, the future is not inevitable; it depends on the choices we make today.","word_count":122,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Rakan's essay. True or False?","items":[{"q":"In 1970, many people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “My Vision for 2050 – Rakan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Rakan says forecasting the future is easy.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “My Vision for 2050 – Rakan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He predicts that electric vehicles are bound to dominate the roads by 2050.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “My Vision for 2050 – Rakan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Rakan thinks humans will probably have colonized Mars by 2050.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “My Vision for 2050 – Rakan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He mentions that longevity will bring new challenges for health systems.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “My Vision for 2050 – Rakan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Rakan believes the future is inevitable.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “My Vision for 2050 – Rakan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"In 1970, many people believed that by 2000 we ___ in cities on the Moon.","options":["would living","are living","would be living","will be living"],"answer":"would be living","explanation":"Future perfect or future in the past.","explanation_ar":"صيغ المستقبل."},{"q":"By 2050, most countries ___ to renewable energy.","options":["would have switch","will have switched","have switched","will switch already"],"answer":"will have switched","explanation":"Future perfect or future in the past.","explanation_ar":"صيغ المستقبل."},{"q":"Electric vehicles are ___ to dominate our roads.","options":["bound","every chance","conceivably","doubtful"],"answer":"bound","explanation":"Probability expression.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال."},{"q":"There is ___ chance that humans will have colonized Mars by 2050.","options":["little","every","a bound","a safe"],"answer":"little","explanation":"Probability expression.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال."},{"q":"Better medicine and healthier diets are increasing human ___.","options":["contingency","paradigm","trajectory","longevity"],"answer":"longevity","explanation":"Futures vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات المستقبل."},{"q":"In the 1960s, many futurists expected that most people would own flying cars by 2000. ______ flying cars remain rare and expensive today.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For this reason,","In reality,","Similarly,","Therefore,"],"answer":"In reality,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare your presentation.","items":[{"q":"A past prediction that failed: ___","options":[],"answer":"In the 1990s, many people thought that paper books would have disappeared by 2020.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A future perfect sentence for 2050: ___","options":[],"answer":"By 2050, most schools will have replaced printed textbooks with interactive ones.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Three probability expressions of different strengths: ___","options":[],"answer":"Teachers are bound to use AI; in all likelihood, classes will be smaller; there's a slim chance that schools will disappear.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A futures word in a sentence: ___","options":[],"answer":"In the worst-case scenario, students will rely too much on technology.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A concluding sentence: ___","options":[],"answer":"Whatever happens, the future of education depends on the choices we make now.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your speculative essay.","items":[{"q":"Plan: hook (past prediction), likely developments, unlikely developments, risks, conclusion.","options":[],"answer":"Hook: 1990s prediction that cash would have disappeared; likely: digital payments, AI doctors; unlikely: Mars colony; risk: privacy; conclusion: our choices","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your speculative essay on the area you chose (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"In the 1990s, some experts predicted that cash would have disappeared completely by 2010. Although digital payments have grown enormously, many people still carry coins and notes. By 2050, however, physical money will in all likelihood have become rare. Most shops will have been accepting only digital payments for years. AI is bound to transform banking, and there is every chance that fraud detection will become faster and more accurate. On the other hand, there is a slim chance that every country will adopt the same digital currency. In the worst-case scenario, people who cannot use technology may be left behind. Whatever happens, the future of money is not inevitable; it depends on the decisions governments and citizens make today.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: future perfect forms correct? past prediction with \"would\"? the grammar after each probability expression? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"there is every chance to fraud detection becomes faster\" to \"there is every chance that fraud detection will become faster\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Quiz Show Challenge","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams take turns choosing a category and a cue word.","Grammar: make a sentence with future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review). Words: give the meaning. Spelling: spell it. Write It!: write a sentence with correct punctuation.","Correct answers score 10 points; teams may steal a question another team misses.","The highest score after 8 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"forecast","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"projection","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"trajectory","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"scenario","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"speculative","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"extrapolate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"inevitable","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"utopia","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"dystopia","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"longevity","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"colonize","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"existential","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"contingency","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"paradigm","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"foresee","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"By the time she turns forty, she ___ in five different countries.","options":["would have lived","has lived","will have lived","will live already"],"answer":"will have lived","explanation":"Future perfect or future in the past.","explanation_ar":"صيغ المستقبل."},{"q":"In the 1980s, my father believed that by 2010 robots ___ all our cooking.","options":["will be doing","would doing","are doing","would be doing"],"answer":"would be doing","explanation":"Future perfect or future in the past.","explanation_ar":"صيغ المستقبل."},{"q":"___, the population of many cities will continue to grow.","options":["Bound to","In all likelihood","The odds against","Every chance"],"answer":"In all likelihood","explanation":"Probability expression.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال."},{"q":"The team drew up a ___ plan in case the launch was delayed.","options":["longevity","dystopia","trajectory","contingency"],"answer":"contingency","explanation":"Futures vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات المستقبل."},{"q":"","options":["The odds is against humans living on Mars by 2035.","The odds are against to humans living on Mars by 2035.","The odds are against that humans live on Mars by 2035.","The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2035."],"answer":"The odds are against humans living on Mars by 2035.","explanation":"Probability expression.","explanation_ar":"الاحتمال."},{"q":"Many people feared that computers would make paper obsolete. ______ global paper use actually increased for several decades after personal computers became common.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Instead,","For example,","Similarly,","Therefore,"],"answer":"Instead,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Solar panels have become much cheaper over the past decade. ______ more households and businesses are installing them.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["As a result,","However,","Nevertheless,","In contrast,"],"answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• In 2023, Rayyanah Barnawi and Ali AlQarni traveled to the International Space Station.\n• They carried out scientific experiments during their mission.\n• Barnawi was the first Saudi woman to travel into space.\n• Saudi Arabia established a national space agency in 2018.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize the historic significance of the 2023 mission. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Saudi Arabia established a space agency in 2018.","The ISS is in space.","The 2023 mission made history when Rayyanah Barnawi became the first Saudi woman to travel into space.","The astronauts carried out experiments."],"answer":"The 2023 mission made history when Rayyanah Barnawi became the first Saudi woman to travel into space.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• UN projections suggest that the world population will reach about 9.7 billion by 2050.\n• Some regions' populations are expected to grow quickly.\n• Others, such as parts of Europe and East Asia, are expected to shrink.\n• Aging populations will need more health care.\n\nThe student wants to show that population change will differ between regions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Aging populations need health care.","While some regions' populations are expected to grow quickly, others, such as parts of Europe and East Asia, are expected to shrink.","The UN makes projections.","The world population may reach 9.7 billion by 2050."],"answer":"While some regions' populations are expected to grow quickly, others, such as parts of Europe and East Asia, are expected to shrink.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best opening sentence for a speculative essay about the future:","options":["By 2050, everything will change already, for sure.","The future is bound happening soon.","In 1970, many people believed that by 2000 we would be living in cities on the Moon.","In 1970, many people believed that by 2000 we will live in cities on the Moon."],"answer":"In 1970, many people believed that by 2000 we would be living in cities on the Moon.","explanation":"Future perfect or future in the past.","explanation_ar":"صيغ المستقبل."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A school \"Futures Fair\": Rakan presenting a poster \"My Vision for 2050\" with a timeline; visitors placing stickers on a probability scale; a model of a green city with solar panels and driverless buses. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A school \"Futures Fair\": Rakan presenting a poster \"My Vision for 2050\" with a timeline; visitors placing stickers on a probability scale; a model of a green city with solar panels and driverless buses"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “The Future of Humanity”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “The Future of Humanity”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L12-U4-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Quiz Show Challenge”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Quiz Show Challenge"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS5-T1","script":"In 1970, many people believed that by 2000 we would be living in cities on the Moon. That prediction failed, which reminds us that forecasting the future is never easy. Even so, some trends make certain outcomes highly probable. By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy, and electric vehicles are bound to dominate our roads. In all likelihood, doctors will have been using AI to diagnose diseases for decades. There is a good chance that people will live longer, but longevity will bring new challenges for health systems. On the other hand, there is little chance that humans will have colonized Mars by then. Whatever happens, the future is not inevitable; it depends on the choices we make today.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U4-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “My Vision for 2050: Language in Use”. Let’s learn future perfect forms and the future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary, advanced rhetorical synthesis; SAT transitions (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Futures fair: prepare a 2-minute presentation of your vision for 2050 in one area (health, cities, space, education, work). Include two future perfect sentences, one past prediction that failed (would have…), three probability expressions of different strengths and three futures words. The audience rates each prediction on a probability scale. Today you use all of Unit 4: the future perfect and future in the past, degrees of probability, futures vocabulary and advanced rhetorical synthesis. A balanced speculative essay begins with a hook (often a past prediction), separates likely from unlikely developments using precise probability language, considers both opportunities and risks, and ends by reminding the reader that the future depends on choices. Transitions such as even so, on the other hand and whatever happens guide the argument. Here are some examples. In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon. By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy. Doctors will have been using AI for decades. Be careful! Some learners say: “By 2050, most countries will switch to renewable energy already.” That’s not right. We say: “By 2050, most countries will have switched to renewable energy.” Future perfect. Quick check: what is wrong with “In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we will live on the Moon.”? … Think! … It should be “In 1970, people believed that by 2000 we would be living on the Moon.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present my vision of the world in 2050.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"By 2040, scientists ___ new treatments for many diseases.","options":["will develop already","have developed","will have developed","would have developed"],"answer":"will have developed","explanation":"Future perfect (will have done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام."},{"q":"By next year, my grandfather ___ his farm for sixty years.","options":["has been running","will have running","will be run","will have been running"],"answer":"will have been running","explanation":"Future perfect continuous (will have been doing).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المستمر."},{"q":"By 2035, the new airport ___.","options":["will have completed","will have been completed","completes","will be complete by"],"answer":"will have been completed","explanation":"Future perfect passive (will have been done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المجهول."},{"q":"In 1900, people imagined that by 2000 everyone ___ in airships.","options":["would be traveling","would traveling","travels","will be traveling"],"answer":"would be traveling","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"The space hotel ___ in 2025, but the project was canceled.","options":["was to opening","will have opened","was to have opened","is to have opened"],"answer":"was to have opened","explanation":"was / were to have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"was to have been."},{"q":"When the first satellites were launched in the 1950s, few engineers could have imagined that, within fifty years, billions of people ______ satellite signals to find their way around cities.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["are using","would be using","will be using","would using"],"answer":"would be using","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"Technology is ___ to change how we learn; there is no doubt.","options":["doubtful","bound","conceivably","every chance"],"answer":"bound","explanation":"Certain: bound to, sure to, foregone conclusion.","explanation_ar":"اليقين."},{"q":"There's every chance ___ the project will succeed.","options":["that","to","of","for"],"answer":"that","explanation":"Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"She stands a good chance of ___ the scholarship.","options":["to win","win","won","winning"],"answer":"winning","explanation":"Grammar after the expression.","explanation_ar":"القواعد بعد التعبير."},{"q":"There's ___ chance that flying cars will be common by 2030.","options":["every","a bound","little","a safe"],"answer":"little","explanation":"Unlikely / impossible: little chance, doubtful, odds against.","explanation_ar":"عدم الترجيح."},{"q":"Because most climate scientists agree that average temperatures will continue to rise, many governments now treat further warming as ______ and are preparing for its effects.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["doubtful","a slim chance","highly speculative","virtually inevitable"],"answer":"virtually inevitable","explanation":"Choose the right degree.","explanation_ar":"درجة الاحتمال."},{"q":"The novel describes a ___ in which people are watched by cameras everywhere.","options":["dystopia","trajectory","longevity","utopia"],"answer":"dystopia","explanation":"Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"In the worst-case ___, coastal cities could flood.","options":["scenario","contingency","paradigm","longevity"],"answer":"scenario","explanation":"Futures collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Few people ___ the rapid rise of social media.","options":["forecasted at","extrapolated to","colonized","foresaw"],"answer":"foresaw","explanation":"Futures word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"By studying temperature records from the past century, scientists can ______ how hot summers are likely to become in the coming decades.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["colonize","foresee as","dystopianize","extrapolate"],"answer":"extrapolate","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The prefix \"fore-\" in \"foresight\" means ___.","options":["before","against","bad","beyond"],"answer":"before","explanation":"Word parts (fore-, extra-, dys-).","explanation_ar":"أجزاء الكلمة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• In the 1990s, many experts predicted that paper books would disappear by 2020.\n• E-readers became popular after 2007.\n• In 2020, printed books still outsold e-books in many countries.\n• Many readers say they prefer the feel of paper.\n\nThe student wants to contrast the experts' prediction with the actual outcome. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Although many experts in the 1990s predicted that paper books would disappear by 2020, printed books still outsold e-books in many countries that year.","E-readers became popular after 2007.","Experts make predictions.","Many readers prefer paper."],"answer":"Although many experts in the 1990s predicted that paper books would disappear by 2020, printed books still outsold e-books in many countries that year.","explanation":"Expectation vs outcome.","explanation_ar":"التوقع والنتيجة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Researchers wanted to find out whether video games improve problem-solving skills.\n• They studied 150 teenagers for one year.\n• Teenagers who played strategy games improved their problem-solving scores.\n• Teenagers who played other games showed no improvement.\n\nThe student wants to present the study's aim and its main finding. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Researchers studied 150 teenagers for one year.","To find out whether video games improve problem-solving skills, researchers studied 150 teenagers and found that only those who played strategy games improved.","Some teenagers played strategy games.","Video games are popular."],"answer":"To find out whether video games improve problem-solving skills, researchers studied 150 teenagers and found that only those who played strategy games improved.","explanation":"Aim, method or finding of a study.","explanation_ar":"الهدف والمنهج والنتيجة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• A new battery charges an electric car in ten minutes.\n• It was tested in a laboratory.\n• The tests used only five cars.\n• The company plans to sell the battery in 2028.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize a limitation of the testing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Electric cars are popular.","The company plans to sell it in 2028.","Although the new battery charged cars in ten minutes, it was tested only in a laboratory using five cars.","The battery charges a car in ten minutes."],"answer":"Although the new battery charged cars in ten minutes, it was tested only in a laboratory using five cars.","explanation":"Limitation.","explanation_ar":"القيد."},{"q":"Many people expected robots to replace most factory workers by now. ______ millions of people still work in factories around the world.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","Therefore,","However,","As a result,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"by 2050 we will reach ten billion people, there's every chance to life expectancy rise.\"","options":["By 2050, we will have reached ten billion people, there's every chance that life expectancy will rise.","By 2050, we will have reached ten billion people, and there's every chance that life expectancy will rise.","By 2050 we will reach ten billion people, and there's every chance that life expectancy will rise.","by 2050, we will have reached ten billion people, and there's every chance that life expectancy will rise."],"answer":"By 2050, we will have reached ten billion people, and there's every chance that life expectancy will rise.","explanation":"Future perfect (will have done).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام."},{"q":"\"In 1960, experts believed that by 2000 we would have built Moon cities.\" Was this prediction fulfilled?","options":["We cannot know.","Yes, partly.","Yes, completely.","No."],"answer":"No.","explanation":"Future in the past (would, would have).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل في الماضي."},{"q":"Which expression shows the highest probability?","options":["might conceivably","is bound to","it's doubtful whether","there's a slim chance"],"answer":"is bound to","explanation":"Choose the right degree.","explanation_ar":"درجة الاحتمال."},{"q":"\"By then, astronauts will have been living in space for fifty years.\" What does the continuous form emphasize?","options":["a single completed action","an arrangement that was canceled","the duration of the activity up to that point","a past habit"],"answer":"the duration of the activity up to that point","explanation":"Future perfect continuous (will have been doing).","explanation_ar":"المستقبل التام المستمر."}],"theme_ar":"مستقبل البشرية","essential_question_ar":"هل يمكننا التنبؤ بالمستقبل، أم الاستعداد له فقط؟","can_do":"I can use the future perfect and future perfect continuous, describe past predictions with the future in the past (would have built, was to have been), express fine degrees of probability (there's every chance, in all likelihood, the odds are), use the vocabulary of futures studies, and answer advanced SAT rhetorical synthesis questions.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أستخدم المستقبل التام والمستقبل التام المستمر، وأصف تنبؤات الماضي بالمستقبل في الماضي (would have built / was to have been)، وأعبّر عن درجات احتمال دقيقة (there's every chance / in all likelihood / the odds are)، وأستخدم مفردات دراسات المستقبل، وأجيب عن أسئلة التركيب البلاغي المتقدمة بنمط SAT."});
